      "pitch": "This document outlines the conceptual framework for the next album by The Shady River Bard, tentatively titled 'Letter to the New Gods' or 'The Empty Throne'. This work serves as the third and culminating installment of a thematic trilogy. The Hammer and the Rust chronicled the dignity of labor and the rise and fall of the industrial heartland.1 Chaff from the Wheat dissected the \"great retraining lie,\" a recurring cynical strategy used to manage the social fallout of technological displacement, from the 19th-century weaver to the 21st-century gig worker.2 Sold Cheap provided an audit of a nation where life itself has been devalued and \"stripped for parts\".3\n\nThose albums chronicled the reign of the \"old gods\"\u2014the 19th-century industrialists like Andrew Ure 2 and the 20th-century corporate-political class that hollowed out the Rust Belt.3 Their reign has ended. They have abdicated, leaving a moral and cultural \"Empty Throne.\" This album is the story of who, and what, has come to claim it.\n\nThe new antagonists are not merely industrialists; they are the high priests of a new, global, secular religion. The tech elite of Silicon Valley are building a \"Cathedral of Code\" 4, a new philosophical and technological framework to house their new god\u2014Artificial General Intelligence\u2014and to achieve their new eschatology: the Singularity.\n\nThis new religion is not a vague notion of \"progress.\" It is a complete, articulated, and self-justifying framework that this album will deconstruct piece by piece. This new gospel includes:",
