      "pitch": "2. The Republic of Nobody \u2014 Alternate title; captures the album's concern with citizens losing meaningful agency.\n\nSanity's Edge is an album about the terrifying collision of a flawed political system, a culture that has normalized permanent war, and a moment in which the person occupying the presidency possesses extraordinary authority over military escalation. The album asks a deliberately uncomfortable question: how much danger can a civilization normalize before the abnormal becomes ordinary\u2014and what happens when the person with the power to make an irreversible decision is himself the subject of serious public questions about judgment, impulse control, cognitive functioning, and fitness for office?\n\nThe research does not establish a clinical diagnosis of Donald Trump, and the album should not pretend that it does. A 2026 BMJ letter explicitly cautions that psychiatric diagnosis requires clinical examination and assessment, even while noting that mental-health professionals have raised concerns about observable behavior. Separately, a May 2026 report described a statement signed by 30 U.S. mental-health specialists declaring Trump unfit to serve and warning about the nuclear danger posed by impaired judgment. The distinction matters artistically: the album is strongest when it dramatizes observable behavior, extraordinary power, institutional safeguards, and consequences\u2014not when it tries to diagnose a person.\n\nThe immediate geopolitical trigger is the 2026 U.S.-Iran war and its continuing escalation. The White House has repeatedly stated that Iran must not obtain a nuclear weapon, while independent arms-control analysis has challenged some of the administration's claims about the immediacy of Iran's nuclear capability. On August 16, former Representative Marjorie Taylor Greene publicly alleged that nuclear use against Iran was being discussed in strategy meetings. She supplied no public evidence, and the White House rejected the allegation. The allegation therefore belongs in the album as a frightening warning and dramatic question\u2014not as an established fact.",
