      "genre": "Celtic Roots Folk",
      "pitch": "Concept Document and Thematic Analysis: The Sovereign's Right\n\nThe following concept document outlines a comprehensive, expert-level blueprint for a full-length concept album to be performed and recorded by the artist known as The Shady River Bard. The proposed working titles for this project are Salvo Jure Cujuslibet, The Stirling Directive, or The Forgotten Crown. The album is designed as a deep, narrative-driven exploration of historical subjugation, the orchestrated division of the working class, and the eventual awakening of a populace to their inherent, unalienable sovereignty. By weaving together the historical realities of the Scottish constitution with the modern struggles of ordinary people, the album will serve as a profound artistic statement on the nature of power, division, and liberation.\n\nThe core concept of the album revolves around the historical and contemporary political landscape of Scotland, specifically addressing the suppression of the Scottish constitution and the 1689 Claim of Right.1 However, consistent with The Shady River Bard's established thematic universe, this is not merely a historical recount. The narrative arc follows a single protagonist\u2014an archivist, historian, or ordinary citizen of the land\u2014who initially observes the slow decay of their community. This decay manifests through widespread poverty, the struggles of the working class, addiction, and the corporate acquisition of public lands. Crucially, the protagonist recognizes that the deep societal divisions among the people\u2014whether driven by partisan politics, economic strife, or social class\u2014are artificially orchestrated. This division is designed by those in power to distract the populace from their shared subjugation and to prevent them from realizing their collective, supreme authority.\n\nAs the protagonist embarks on a journey of historical discovery, they unearth the suppressed truths of the 1707 Treaty of Union, which effectively annexed the nation under the guise of a voluntary partnership.2 Delving deeper into the archives, the protagonist discovers the ancient legal and moral mechanism of salvo jure cujuslibet (saving the right of anyone) 4, a principle proving that the people's rights cannot be lawfully extinguished by the state. The journey culminates in a mobilization of the spirit, mirroring modern efforts to seek international recognition for decolonization through the United Nations.6 The ultimate message of the album is a profound declaration that sovereignty rests with the people, not with parliaments or crowns, and that healing contemporary societal division is the necessary precursor to reclaiming that sovereignty. Only by standing together can the people enforce their constitution.",
